    <title size="LARGER" v="v1">
        <textual>Icons</textual>
    </title>

    <text v="v1">
        <textual>
            If you want to use Icons in your style pages, you need to enable them in the iconConfig.json file. Set
            enable to true, and they will be available. An icon system will have to be included in the Materials in
            order for this to work.
        </textual>
    </text>
    <text v="v1">
        <textual>
            So how do Icons work? We are going to take Bootstrap Icons as an example. But you can use whatever icon
            system you want. Bootstrap Icons can be found using the link below. When rendering an icon, the icon.ftl
            template is passed a name token. It's up to you to make that name token work with whatever icon system you
            are using. When a user adds an Icon via the Admin Console he provides the name.
        </textual>
        <link type="external" url="https://icons.getbootstrap.com/" openNewWindow="true" v="v1">
            <textual>Bootstrap</textual>
        </link>
    </text>
    <text v="v1">
        <textual>
            In order for the user to understand what he must enter for the name property, there is a bit of html we
            present to the user in the Admin Console that is supplied here in the Materials. This html is defined in the
            iconHelp.html file.
        </textual>
    </text>

    <title size="LARGER" v="v1">
        <textual>Images</textual>
    </title>

    <text v="v1">
        <textual>
            All images that are available to the user are supplied via the Materials. There needs to be a list of images
            for the Admin Console to know what to present to the user in the Image Editor. This list is the imges.json
            file.
        </textual>
    </text>

    <title size="LARGER" v="v1">
        <textual>Emails</textual>
    </title>

    <text v="v1">
        <textual>
            Emails are very similar to Pages. In the emails directory you will find email.xml files. These files are the
            definitions for Emails that are predefined. Additional Emails can be added via the Admin Console. What is
            necessary to understand from a Materials standpoint is how to correctly configure how images are attached to
            emails during the email sending process. Emails, unlike web pages, mostly embed css and images. This allows
            the email to be viewed offline. The email.properties file contains a list of attachments which is a comma
            separated list of images that need to be embedded with each email.
        </textual>
    </text>

    <title size="LARGER" v="v1">
        <textual>Plantain Substitutions</textual>
    </title>

    <text v="v1">
        <textual>
            In order for the Materials Toolkit to work, it needs some system for handling Plantain Scripts. The
            Materials Toolkit does not include a Plantain Engine. Instead, if the Materials Toolkit finds a script, it
            will look for a result in the plantain-substitutions.json file. This makes better sense as it is a lot
            easier to test conditions. You can see an example of this in the specials page where we demonstrate how the
            Switch Widget works. You will see that the Plantain Scripts found for the condition attributes on the If
            Widgets can be found in the substitutions file.
        </textual>
    </text>
</page>